In article <sb380.769076379@victoria>, HOLT A <sb380@city.ac.uk> wrote:
>kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>>q: Should university's create a rule banning "porn" on university
>>computers?
>>a: No, such a rule would be unnecessary and too broad.
>However, in some countries it may be necessary:
>  In some countries where there is a clear and consistent law
>  (probably including most "fundamentalist" Islamic states) such
>  a rule is unnecessary as the law will cover the situation (but
>  a reminder of the law may well be a sensible precaution).
>  In countries like England (and, from what I hear, Canada) where
>  the definition of pornographic is variable according to the feelings
>  and where "possession for distribution" - even if knowledge is not
>  proved - is a serious offence, it is advisable to have a specific
>  rule. 

How will that help?

There is no definition of "obscene" under UK law.  It's up to the
courts to decide, often on a whim.  Works of art have been banned and
people prosecuted; sexually explicit video tapes have been allowed.

No university-imposed rule will provide immunity from prosecution, as
no university can predict what the law courts will decide is or is not
pornographic.  Unless, that is, you're prepared to ban many works of
art such as the ones Carl cited.

>As UK law stands at the moment, _Playboy_ centrefolds or reproductions
>from art collections would generally not be considered illegal - but some
>of the more explicit images that can be found in obscure places on the
>net or on some CDROMs definitely would be.

Maybe, maybe not.  The Robert Mapplethorpe photos which caused such a
fuss in the US were shown in the UK without incident.  On the other
hand, some Jeff Koons work which was shown in the US without incident
was the subject of a successful prosecution under the obscene
publications act over here.

There's no law, no guideline, and hence no way to predict what will be
ruled obscene in the UK.  You can only guess.  And what's the point of
having a policy which is pure guesswork?

>This is not a sexual harassment issue. Few UK Universities are likely to
>have Playboy in the library unless.

Eh?  Cambridge University has every issue of Playboy in the library, and
many other "adult" magazines besides.  Some of the more "naughty" stuff
is only available if specifically requested by someone more senior than
an undergraduate, but it's all there.  (I know someone who had to look
up an article in a girlie mag as part of his PhD research. (*))

I know that the CU library is a copyright library, and so it's a
little different from those at other universities, but I don't think
the situation's *that* different elsewhere.

>there is an important difference - material in the library is purchased
>from limited funds on the basis of selection by recommendation from 
>members of the academic staff (who can usually be expected to show
>some level of reponsibility).

"Responsibility"?  I thought academic staff were supposed to show
responsibility towards the academic ideals of free intellectual
discourse and the freedom of ideas, not responsibility to pro-actively
censor material which some people find objectionable.


mathew
