In article <2r6svt$dl7@zippo.uwasa.fi> ts@uwasa.fi (Timo Salmi) writes:
>In article <2r6nll$5uf@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>:I just created this new Computers and Academic Freedom FAQ. Please
>:post or email any comments or corrections.
>FAQ?  That is not a FAQ.  It looks more like an opinion from someone

	It addresses more a "Question" ("What should the policy be
regarding ...")  than a "question" ("Where can I find sample policy
regarding ..."). Frequently Discussed Issues are commonly within the
purview of FAQ, so I think the objection above is based on an
interpretation of FAQ that is far too restrictive. There's little
question it's not "objective", but it's dealing with an issue more
social than technical. A FAQ could be written from a different
viewpoint, but I'd say it's still worthy of the title "FAQ".

	I think the document is good and helpful. If anything, I would
suggest it be stronger and have more references (this may be something
to consider for future revisions). The general tone of it is "Computers
should be considered like any other media in terms of policy". This is
quite right, in my opinion, but I'm a little concerned about the way
that it's argued. Perhaps I'm criticizing beyond it's scope, but I'm
afraid that a position ONLY of "treat electronic civil liberties as
other media" is vulnerable to attack of "first establish that such and
such is not a civil liberty when done on computers (easier, since the
laws here are less clear, and resource control more arbitrary and
centralized), and so, since electronic media are no different from
others, the prohibitions should be acceptable elsewhere." Essentially, a
slippery slope applied from electrons to paper. Advocates of broad
restrictions may try to have the initial interpretations of local rules
be made onerous in newer areas, then try to apply that censorship to
areas where protections are better established.
	Let me make this clear with an example:

	While you may intend only to stop computer-science student from
	looking [at] _Playboy_ centerfolds in your computer labs, your
	rule may also stop liberal-arts students from viewing the
	growing number of fine art collections on the Net.

	This leaves an impression (though such may not be the intent)
that it wouldn't be a problem to have a rule against looking at
_Playboy_ centerfolds. I'll go out on limb, and say that such a narrow
rule would still be very dangerous and problematic from a
civil-liberties perspective (leaving aside issues as to whether would in
fact be legal or not). The quote comes to mind about the First Amendment
leaving matters of taste and style up to the individual, protecting both
low and high art. What happens when _Playboy_ sets up a site on the
World Wide Web? Will it against the rule to contact it?
	Now, if it becomes accepted that some material cannot be seen on
a computer terminal, what is to constrain people from arguing "This wouldn't
be permitted on a computer screen, shouldn't it be a similar offense for
people to read it on paper in public where others might see it?" (the
reverse of the argument we usually make on paper=>screen). Arguments
about reading _Playboy_ in public constituting sexual harassment have in
fact been made (see Nat Hentoff's excellent book, _Free Speech for
Me-But Not for Thee_, page 14, note Hentoff is a "Village Voice"
columnist, not a right-wing exaggerator), so this is not wild speculation.
	While "Computer sites should publicize the university's sexual
harassment rules; they should not try to preempt them." is good as far
as it goes, realize that many such rules are vague and unclear.  The
classical "chilling effect" is perhaps more in evidence due to the newer
nature of electronic media. I suppose the bottom line is the following:
Since computers are a just now becoming a "mass media", and have a
special vulnerability in this situation, I suggest you include more
material at the primary (as opposed to reference) level focusing on the
civil-liberties problems of overly broad policies and resources to argue
against typical speech, expression, reading, and viewing restrictions.

--
