>Usenet is funded by government, and by various institutions.

True. It is funded by the sites that subscribe to it. Some
sites are government, some are private.

>It is not strictly speaking a public forum, since it costs money to
>use.

The fact that it costs money for some people to access Usenet is
irrelevent. The Usenet facilities are my university (the U. of
Illinois) are a public forum because 1. the U. of Illinois is a state
university and 2. the U. of Illinois runs its Usenet facilities as a
forum. The Usenet facilities are USC are not a public forum because
USC is a private university.

>There is no line that needs to be drawn, nor is the censoring of
>certain views illegal.

Depends, censorship might be illegal a least 3 ways:

  1. Censorship might violate the Constitution.

       In a public forum (e.g. the typical Usenet facility at a state
       university), it is illegal to, for example, censor on the basis
       of viewpoint. In other words, if the state allows someone to
       write "cats are great" in a public forum, it is illegal to
       punish someone who writes "cats are terrible". USC, however,
       is not a state university, so any censorship by it would not be
       unconsititional.

  2. Censorship might violate statute.

       In my state, Illinois, there is no law that prohibits private
       universities from censoring their students. California, however,
       has SB 1115, 1992, a state law that says:

         "(Chapter 3.3, Section 94367) Private postsecondary
         educational institutions shall not make or enforce any
         rule subjecting any student to disciplinary sanctions
         solely on the basis of conduct that is speech or other
         communication that, when engaged in outside the campus
         or facility of a private postsecondary institution, is
         protected from governmental restriction by the First
         Amendment to the United States Consitituion or Section 2
         of Article 1 of the California Constitution."

  3. Censorship might violate contract.

       The student code at a university is part of a legally enforceable
       contract between school and student. If it says "Students will
       not be subject to institutional censorship", then it is
       illegal to subject them to institutional censorship.
       Most U.S. schools do promise not to censor students even
       on campus.

>If you want full rights to say what you'd like, start a bulletin board,
>and allow anyone to call in. The Internet is non-profit by tradition, and
>open to ideas (people form charters for new newsgroups). Each newsgroup
>though is not open to any topic, since each has been drafted with a specific
>purpose. Violating that charter, violates the premise of the usenet. That
>is defrauding the corporations and universities that spend money on the usenet.

"Defrauding"????? I don't think so. Care to quote which statue
outlaws this kind of "fraud"?

While some parts of some charters may be enforceable by university
authorities (if they give notice), it would be illegal for a
university to enforce other parts. For example, there used to be a
women-only newsgroup. It would be illegal for a university to punish
men who joins it. Likewise, it is illegal for state universities and
California universities to discriminate in a public forum on the basis
of viewpoint.

- Carl

