> Pardon my ignorance, but what is BrisNet?  

"Brisbane Public Access Network Assocation Incorporated".  A non-profit
association which provides Internet access to the public in Brisbane.

> Assuming that it is, indeed, a private business, I'm a bit uncomfortable
> with these comments:

> >Organisations like my own are dependent on the universities for helping to
> >share the costs of news feeds, but we have absolutely no say in what those
> >universities are required to provide.  I want this situation changed,
> >especially in light of volume charging which will urge the universities
> >to cut the feed unless they are required to provide it.

> It sounds like "I'm making a profit from the university's news feed, so I
> want a say in what they do."  

Thing is, I'm not making a profit.  Going out backwards might be a more
accurate description. :-)  Even if BrisNet was managing to cover costs with
a little on the side, BrisNet's members don't personally make any money
from it.  All funds (such as they are) go back into supporting public
access in my area.

Maybe eventually, a commercial version of BrisNet will arise, but we'll need
a lot more capital than we have now.  Heck, we may even be able to afford to
import our own interstate news feed eventually if we get a massive increase
in membership.  But, in the meantime, small non-profit networks like my own
are dependent on the local university's good graces.  If those good graces
dry up, not only am I stuffed, but any future startup network as well.

Membership of public access networks in Australia is measured in the tens
and hundreds, not the thousands as in the US.  We're probably about 3-5
years behind you in the development of public access.

Cheers,

Rhys.

