: How will that help?

: There is no definition of "obscene" under UK law.  It's up to the
: courts to decide, often on a whim.  Works of art have been banned and
: people prosecuted; sexually explicit video tapes have been allowed.

: No university-imposed rule will provide immunity from prosecution, as
: no university can predict what the law courts will decide is or is not
: pornographic.  Unless, that is, you're prepared to ban many works of
: art such as the ones Carl cited.

This is no different than the case in the U.S.  There is no way for
someone to know whether a work violates community standards prior to
publication.  The U.S. Supreme Court made a muddle of free speech in
California v. Miller.  Brennan, the author of the majority opinion in the
case later reversed himself for just that reason.  That is also why
Justice Byron White once quipped he could not defin obscenity but he knew
it when he saw it.

The concept of community standards means there is no standard.  One of the
reasons that the Bill of Rights was included in the Constitution was to
protect free expression from the vagueries of both state and federal
government.  If you doubt this, take a look at some of the books and works
of art that graced the shelves of our founders.  They were not puritans or
blue noses.  It is interesting to note that the government did not bother
with concepts like obscenity until the Victorian age when professional
busy bodies like Comstock started braying about loose moral standards.  A
pity the U.S. Supreme Court could not leave well enough alone.

Mitch

