Melinda Shore (shore@dinah.tc.cornell.edu) wrote:
: In article <2r1p8i$3po@gibeah.connected.com> warrl@gibeah.connected.com (Donald Edwards) writes:
: >Here you see why a self-respecting and foresighted [public] library should not
: >rely on government for funding.

: Absolutely!  No private funding source would EVER reduce
: its support.  Unlike our mean old nasty stingy local
: governments, they're all far too noble and generous to even
: THINK about letting libraries rot.

IMHO you are being either deliberately obnoxious or incredibly dense.

Imagine for the sake of argument that the primary source of funding for a
certain library decides to ban books by a certain author.  

If it is a public library, the primary source of funding is probably the
local city council.  This body has the authority to *shut* *down* the
library, if necessary, to enforce the ban.  (Of course it'll probably
lose the subsequent court battles, but that will take a while and be
expensive.)

If it is a private library, the primary source of funding is a certain
donor.  He or she may choose not to donate, but *cannot* force the library
to close.  Depending on the form of the support, he or she may succeed
in forcing the library to move -- but cannot force it to close.  And the
loss of support from this one source can be offset by additional support
from other sources.

Which has more autonomy?

On the other hand, imagine for the sake of argument that a library
receives a large bequest which is to be spent for a new building.

If it is a public library, it cannot build the new building -- even though
it has all the necessary funding from a separate source -- without the
consent of the politicians.  This consent is likely to come with strings
attached, additional requirements which have nothing to do with the
library.  Our local library got hit with a requirement that it have a
meeting room of a certain size, substantially larger than the City Council
chambers, and be on a specific site right next to City Hall.  The purpose
of this was to provide a larger Council Chamber.  Most of this town's
residents have no idea where City Hall is, as it is on a back street; the
old Library site, and several other sites considered, were much more
suitable and accessible to the public.  But they built on this lousy
location and provided a meeting room quite adequately large to house
the entire book collection.  No alternative.

If on the other hand a private library receives such a bequest, the 
library board can do what is appropriate for the library -- not for the
City Council.
 

