Yep.  But that is the point -- the Miller test is screwed in the first
place.

>Because universities and libraries mostly only collect material of
>"serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value" it makes
>sense to exempt them from obscenity statutes.

Not really.  If their material is REALLY within that guideline, it is not
obscene in the first place.  Therefore, no exception is needed.

On the other hand, if it is license to procure and distribute otherwise
obscene material....

>Adult book stores, on the other hand, pretty offer material *because*
>of its prurient value so it makes no sense to automatically exempt
>them from obscenity statutes.

Doesn't make any difference.  The point is NOT where you buy or look at it,
the point is whether it fails the Miller test in the first place. 

The locale of the viewing is NOT relavent to the Miller test.

>Should universities and libraries really refuse exemptions in solitary
>with adult book stores? Would you really refuse an exemption for
>general, public-access computer services in solitary with adult book
>stores?

Nobody in their right mind would refuse an exemption.  However, the rest of
us who are NOT exempt can work like mad to get the exemption repealed,
which will bring the howls of protest that SHOULD be heard in the first
place on the original issue.

