Message-ID: <1994May22.215534.15711@news.qut.edu.au>
Date: Sun, 22 May 94 21:55:34 GMT

Last week I received a letter from the Registrar of The University of
Melbourne in reply to my previous letter requesting information on the
withdrawl of certain sex-related newsgroups.  I've thought long and hard
before posting this, which is manifestly a private letter, but have
decided to stick my neck out.  If the University feels wronged by my
actions, they need only contact me and I'll post a public apology.

Any transcription errors are my own.  My comments follow the letter.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
20-300-1					11 May, 1994

Mr R Weatherley
President
Brisbane Public Access Network
 Association Incorporated (BrisNet)
5 Horizon Drive
JAMBOREE HEIGHTS  QLD  4074

Dear Mr Weatherley,

	Thank you for your letter of 4 May, 1994 concerning the decision by
AARNet to discontinue certain news feeds.  I suspect that you have sent your
letter to the incorrect address since The University of Melbourne operates
the AARNet hub and Internet link under contract from AARNet.  I am, therefore,
unable to pursue the matter of your complaint.

	It is probably important, however, that I point out to you that the
misuse of communication facilities in the terms of the relevant Commonwealth
acts is not something which any institution can sanction and it is, therefore,
unlikely that any University will knowingly take feeds of the news groups
you mention.  You note in your letter a number of principles which
University's are sworn to uphold; they are also expected to be good corporate
citizens.

					Yours sincerely,

					JB Potter
					Registrar
-------------------------------------------------------------------------

First up, in my letter I referred to a policy instituted by the Registrar
of Information Technology, not a decision made by AARNet.  So, is this guy
the Registrar of Information Technology, or only the more general University
Registrar?  Alternatively, did AARNet put pressure on the University to
remove these newsgroups?  I think not based on Geoff's previous comments.

The Registrar's reply seems to be a case of passing the buck to AARNet.
So Geoff, would you like to pass it straight back to him? :-)  Maybe he
is just confused about the difference between running the AARNet hub and
providing a news feed.  There's a lot of that going around lately. :-)

As to the reference to Commonwealth acts, he did not actually cite which
acts would have been violated, which makes me suspect that he has heard
rumors of such things, but has not done the actual legal ground-work himself
(or delegated the ground-work as appropriate).  Rumors of what acts cover
can be just as dangerous to freedom of speech as blatant censorship.  Then
again, maybe he has done the ground-work.  If we can pin down exactly which
acts the Registrar feels would be violated, then we'll have a fixed target
that we can attack and lobby the government to change.  We can also point
out misinterpretations to the Registrar as necessary.

He also didn't address my main point which was to question why the
University of Melbourne's library catalog has thousands of references to
sex-related topics, yet the same information cannot be carried on the
university's computer system.  If the acts he alluded to do indeed restrict
what can be made available on the computer system, then it does not bode
well for future electronic libraries.  "If it's paper it's allowed, but
if it's digitial it's not".  It also could have a chilling effect on
academic freedom: a researcher who posts a paper discussing sex-related
topics to a newsgroup frequented by collegues could be in violation of
the communications laws!

I'll probably have another go at him, but wanted to get some feedback from
the net and AARNet first.

If you want a copy of my original letter, then e-mail me.

Cheers,

Rhys.
