Or that a totalitarian might realize that not all libraries are funded
by school boards, or that a totalitarian might realize that the specific
form efforts at censorship have most often taken in the recent past might
not necessarily be the only possible forms.

Or that when a person logically shows how something is true, simply
asserting without argument that it is false is not constructive.


: A libertarian who knows something about eaotlaoh would
: probably be aware that there really isn't any legal
: recourse when a private funding source refuses to pay for
: the purchase of, say Ayn Rand novels. 

A totalitarian thinks that one person should be required to do
with his resources what some other specified entity wants done with
those resources.  A libertarin believes that it is quite within John
Doe's rights to specify that *his* donation not be used to buy Ayn
Rand novels... and for the library to instead use someone else's
donation for that purpose.

: John Crerar's
: bequest to found a library in Chicago specified "no
: skeptical trash or dirty French novels," so a science
: library was built, without regard for whether or not it was
: what was most needed in Chicago at that time.

John Crerar's bequest to found a library in Chicago didn't specify
that the library which was built be what was most needed in Chicago
at the time.  The agency receiving this bequest was free to refuse
the bequest... or to spend it any way they liked, *provided* that it
be consistent with the terms of the bequest.  The money was John's,
not theirs, to distribute according to his wishes.  He had no
obligation to leave one dime for the purpose of founding libraries.
A totalitarian won't understand this, of course.

  And while
: the John Crerar Library may have books claiming that the
: Ark of the Covenant is a radio and that the cherubim are
: antennae, they don't have any dirty French novels.

: Libertarianism is attractive because it allows one to
: sound principled and to exercise one's own greed without
: actually having to think very hard,

that's right.  Consistent Libertarians actually have to *earn* their
wealth, which means *persuade* someone to *voluntarily* give it to
them.  Perhaps by offering something in trade, which the Libertarian
thinks is less valuable to him than what he wants but the *other* party
finds *more* valuable to *him*.  Therefore, a libertarian need only think
"what do I have that I want less than that, which that other personw ould
want more than they want that?"  This is of course painfully obvious in
all cases.

Whereas the consistent totalitarian has to exercise the severe mental
strain involved in holding someone up at gunpoint.

: but it's pretty rare
: that libertarian ideals can be used to solve real-world
: problems.  

Here you are correct.  Libertarian ideals do not work well in solving
the problems of totalitarianism, unless the totalitarians can first
be removed from the scene -- or at least reduced substantially in
influence.  Since this all-too-often requires the use of force, and
even most libertarians don't really understand how pervasive force is
in our society (particularly when the government is involved), even
those who believe they have a right and duty to take up arms against
the government under certain circumstances are not likely to recognise
when those circumstances occur.
 

